# Line endings.
#
# EVERYTHING IS LF UNLESS A LATER RULE SAYS OTHERWISE. This matters here for
# three concrete reasons rather than for tidiness:
#
#   1. `plugin/` is vendored into the vncode pack by `scripts/sync-to-vncode.mjs`,
#      and BOTH sides of that copy are compared byte for byte (`--check` fails on
#      any difference). With `core.autocrlf=true` a Windows checkout materialises
#      these files as CRLF while the commit holds LF, so the sync would report
#      drift on every file on exactly the machine it runs on.
#   2. The pack's own distribution records a sha256 per shipped file, and a
#      copied file whose line endings depend on who checked it out cannot be
#      verified against the commit it came from.
#   3. `scripts/install.sh` is a shebang script: a CRLF shebang does not run.
#
# LF is what git STORES either way - it normalises on commit - so this rule only
# stops the checkout from disagreeing with the commit.
* text=auto eol=lf

# A shebang script, explicit even though the catch-all covers it: it is the rule
# that must not be lost if the catch-all is ever narrowed.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The browser bundle is a classic script the harness reads as bytes and the
# pack's checks compare against the source repository.
plugin/lib/client.js text eol=lf

# Batch and cmd files are the Windows launchers, and cmd.exe wants CRLF. The
# LAST matching rule wins, so these sit below the catch-all on purpose.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
